@section ffstreamer

Read an input whose every byte a parent process serves, by offset, over two
pipes it hands down. FFmpeg confined by a sandbox can then read its input with
no path to the file, no socket and no other way to reach the storage: the
parent decides every read, and storage that stops answering holds the parent,
which can always end the run, rather than FFmpeg waiting in the kernel.

The parent speaks the ffstreamer protocol, specified at
@url{https://github.com/kstenerud/ffmpeg-ffstreamer}. It creates the two pipes,
hands FFmpeg one end of each, and names them with the options below. The URL is
exactly @code{ffstreamer:}, with nothing after it. Reading only; each pipe
serves one session at a time.

This protocol accepts the following options:

@table @option
@item ffstreamer_read_fd
The descriptor the parent's messages arrive on. Required.

@item ffstreamer_write_fd
The descriptor this side's messages go out on. Required.

@item ffstreamer_cache_blocks
How many of the parent's blocks to keep, from 2 to 1024. Default 32.
@end table

The parent should restrict the input to this protocol, since a nested open
could otherwise name one of the channel's descriptors with @code{pipe:} or
@code{fd:}:
@example
ffmpeg -protocol_whitelist ffstreamer -ffstreamer_read_fd 4 -ffstreamer_write_fd 5 -i ffstreamer: ...
@end example

